Write a source directive above the declaration or statement it affects. In a
package facade, you can use `$export` to publish a name and `$doc` to record
what it means.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- @package.moth
    $export $doc [:The greeting shown to new users.]
    greeting #= "Hello"
]

Both directives apply to `greeting`. You can put them on consecutive lines or
separate them with a space on one line. The declaration starts on the next
line. A blank line breaks the attachment. Each directive name can appear once
on the same target.

### Build inputs and notes

`$config` marks an explicitly typed compile-time binding as a build input.
In this example `"en"` supplies the fallback value.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    language #String = "en"
]

`$todo` takes a compile-time String, like `$doc`. Use it to annotate a
declaration or a complete statement. The compiler checks the text without
emitting any output for the annotation. `$doc` keeps its text as declaration
documentation.

### Operands

Each directive defines which operand it accepts. Write the operand after a
space. For example, `$export @forms` publishes a name under the facade's
`forms` route. On its own, `$export` publishes at the facade root. Inside a template,
directives go in the head with commas between items. See @../template-directives (Template directives) or
@../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build inputs).
